RES osdtriplet
! ---- RIM + SETWV / SETDENS / SSI + GO ----
RIM
SETWV 1
SETDENS 32
SSI 50
GO
! ---- MTF + MFR / IFR + GO ----
MTF
MFR 200
IFR 5
GO
! ---- PLTRMS + RMSDATA + GO ----
PLTRMS
RMSDATA WAVE
GO
! ---- ZERN_TST + SETZERNC + GO ----
ZERN_TST
SETZERNC 4..8
GO
! ---- SPDSSI + SPO + GO ----
SPDSSI 5.0
SPO
GO
! ---- XOFF / YOFF / VIEOFF + VIE + GO ----
! Note: VIEOVER (overlay flag) is tested standalone in gui_guards.zoa; a VIE
! that actually consumes the overlay crashes headlessly (no NEUTRAL.DAT buffer).
XOFF 10
YOFF 10
VIEOFF 0 0 0
VIE ; GO
! ---- FAN at base level → deterministic error golden ----
FAN
! ---- TFMOTION reports current focus-motion settings ----
TFMOTION
! ---- PLTRMS spot sampling is pinned to its own Density setting ----
! Kept last so it does not renumber the plots above.  The spot branch drives
! SPD, whose sampling lives in mutable KDP globals.  These two runs bracket a
! global change: the RMS numbers must come out identical, and the trailing SPD
! must still report the default 861-ray ring pattern (i.e. PLTRMS restored the
! globals it borrowed).
PLTRMS
RMSDATA SPOT
NUMPTS 3
SETDENS 16
GO
SPOT RECT
RECT 30
PLTRMS P2
RMSDATA SPOT
NUMPTS 3
SETDENS 16
GO
SPOT RING
FOB 0
SPD
! ---- RSPH: per-plot Reference setting, and the legacy global ----
! As a plot setting it must change this plot's wavefront numbers and then be
! restored: the ZERN_TST that follows reads the same REFLOC global and must
! come back with tilt/focus intact (Z3/Z4 non-zero).  Bare RSPH then reports
! CHIEF, and at top level RSPH must still set the global the legacy way --
! the ZERN_TST after it has Z3/Z4 driven to ~0.
PLTRMS P3
RMSDATA WAVE
NUMPTS 3
RSPH BEST
GO
ZERN_TST
NUMPTS 2
SETZERNC 1..4
GO
RSPH
RSPH BEST
ZERN_TST P2
NUMPTS 2
SETZERNC 1..4
GO
RSPH BOGUS
RSPH CHIEF
! ---- AIRY: Airy disk overlay on the spot diagram ----
! Kept last so it does not renumber the plots above.  Radius is
! 1.22*lambda*F/#: for osdtriplet that is 1.22 * 0.58756um * F/5.0011 =
! 0.003585 mm, which the Data tab reports.
SPO P4
AIRY ON
GO
